today on campaign check the  Conservatives plans for social care  Boris Johnson was very clear when he  took office that it was a priority we  will fix the crisis in social care once  and for all with a clear plan we have  prepared to give every older person the  dignity and security they deserve that  was then but do natori still have a plan  and what is it well not according to  their manifesto which only commits to  seek cross-party support for an  unspecified plan that would mean no one  has to sell their home to pay for care  it also promises an extra billion pounds  a year to tackle the short-term funding  crisis now Social Care matters because  we're all living longer by 2060 620  point 4 million people will be over 65  that's one in four of the population and  Social Care is means-tested so only  those with total assets of less than 23  thousand two hundred and fifty pounds  receive free care which leaves the  majority facing a lottery with  potentially huge costs for conditions  like dementia and in the last 20 years  more than three hundred and thirty  thousand people have had to sell their  homes to pay for care now this chart  shows Social Care spending since 2010  and the impact the extra 1 billion  pounds would have over the next four  years you can see it's still three point  six billion pounds short of keeping up  with rising demand and wages and it is  way off the eleven point seven billion  pounds required to restore Social Care  Services to pre austerity levels now for  context we can compare it with the other  party's promises there's the  Conservatives 1 billion pounds this is  the Liberal Democrats promising 2.9  billion pounds but even Labour's offer  of 11 point 1 billion isn't enough to  drag standards back to 2010 levels now  the central point the conservative  manifesto avoids is that if homeowners  are to be protected and somebody else  has got to pay Boris Johnson has ruled  out raising income tax so one option  might be compulsory social insurance  that's effectively a new tax you can  impose a cap on how much people would  have to pay or national insurance could  be extended to pension income but all of  these involve difficult choices and the  Conservatives remember well how to read  Mays election campaign was derailed by  this issue there is no cheap simple  answer and in this election the  Conservatives haven't really tried to  find one solving the social care crisis  is as much a question of political  appetite as of economics
 

